Yes, it is possible to fake entire WebSocket communication without a real server.

You can achieve this by using `page.routeWebSocket()` or `browserContext.routeWebSocket()`. By default, when you set up a route and do not call `webSocketRoute.connectToServer()`, Playwright assumes the WebSocket is being mocked and will not attempt to connect to a real server.

To mock the communication, use the `ws.onMessage()` method to listen for messages from the page and `ws.send()` to respond to them.

**Example:**
```javascript
await page.routeWebSocket('wss://example.com/ws', ws => {
  ws.onMessage(message => {
    if (message === 'request') {
      ws.send('response');
    }
  });
});
```

In this setup, Playwright automatically opens the WebSocket inside the page for you.

